Think Tanks:-

Worried:-

- Ideologically driven
- Fabian society - centre left - labour
- IEA - libertarian - Truss Tory

Not Worried:-

-  Some are neutral experts
    - Give government genuinely helpful neutral information
    - Chatham House - global politics analysis
    - Demos - development of social policy
- Politicians can ignore them
    - Taxpay
    
Corporations:-

Worried:-

-  Corporations using politicians - Meta appointed Nick Clegg as their president of global affairs. His insider connections will allow Meta to influence policy.
    - Unfair insider access
- Church of England and universities do similar

Not Worried:-

- Meta represents the people that use their services - the corporation does not share the interests of the people

Lobbyists :- 

Worried:-

- ‘Revolving doors’
    - Stephen Byers, Geoff Hoon and Patricia Hewitt were suspended in 2010 from the Labour party for accepting an offer to work for a fake consultancy firm for £5000/day
    - Cameron working for Greensill capital and Greensill working for Cameron
- Over representation of small voices in society - Raptor Alliance paid to change policy. Tiny organisation

Not Worried:-

- Unfairly criticised for a small number of cases
- Broaden the debate by letting all people be heard
    - Raptor Alliance
    - Gin drinkers/makers
- Raptor alliance - saved racing pigeons from birds of prey by lobbying parliament
- Lobbyists got the gin act of 1751 repealed - new exciting industry


Are pressure groups good for society:-

- Widespread participation
- Freedom of information and expression
- Protection of rights and liberties
- Pluralism

- Pressure groups are therefore good for democracy because…
- They allow different people to have their view heard by government
- They provide the government with information and statistics that it might otherwise not discover
- They allow people to focus on one issue which really concerns them (e.g. the environment) but which might not be their prime concern in an election
- Pressure groups allow people to participate in democracy at any time, rather than just during an election campaign every 5 years

Pressure groups are undemocratic: they allow those with the loudest voice to be heard most - the elites (rich, educated, articulate and well-connected)

- Pressure group leaders are often middle class
- pressure-groups often suffer from poor internal democracy (top-down decisions, with little consultation), so they cannot be said to truly represent the views of their members
- Conservative Douglas Hurd referred to [[pressure-groups]] as ‘serpents that strangle efficient government’, and Thatcher wanted to reduce the power of trade unions, because of the belief that pressure groups hamper elected governments, preventing them from carrying out their democratic mandate, and forcing them to focus on the interests of one section of society without being able to do ‘joined up thinking’ about how this might affect the bigger picture
